# INTENTIONAL DIVERGENCE. `+=` in Oro is **pure rebinding**: `x += y` is exactly
# `x = x + y`, for every type, always. Python's list `+=` mutates in place via
# `__iadd__`, so an aliased list changes under the alias; Oro removed every
# in-place collection op precisely to kill that bug class, so `b` below stays
# what it was. This is the one place augmented assignment's behaviour is visible
# and it is the reason completing the operator set does not reintroduce the trap.
a = [1, 2]
b = a
a += [3]
print("a", a)
print("b", b)
# Same for every collection-ish op, so the whole family rebinds:
xs = [1]
ys = xs
xs *= 3
print("xs", xs)
print("ys", ys)
